<!--
  This peer answers the REGISTER and the message-waiting SUBSCRIBE of a DUT on
  a reliable transport, each with 200 and a 3600-second expiry.

  RFC 3842 names the event package, and RFC 6665 section 4.1.2.1 the exchange.
  A 200 with an Expires moves the subscription to its active state. This peer
  echoes the Contact in each 200.

  One scenario must answer both requests. The DUT subscribes only from a
  registered line (`src/proto/sip/account.c`, `account_sub_start` runs from the
  registration callback), so this scenario must answer the registration. SIPp
  takes an out-of-call scenario in UAC mode alone, so a UAS run has exactly
  one scenario for every request that reaches it. The two requests carry
  different Call-IDs and open two instances of this one scenario. The first
  receive therefore matches a regular expression over the two method names
  (SIPp 3.7.3 `call.cpp`, `matches_scenario` with `regexp_match`).

  The Contact is echoed, not written, as `test/e2e/register_ooc.xml` echoes
  it: a registrar answers the binding that it was given.

  It sends no NOTIFY, on purpose. The message-waiting intake has its own
  scenario and its own flow (`uas_mwi_notify.xml`), and this scenario exists
  for the SUBSCRIBE itself. A subscription that the notifier accepted is
  active on the 2xx alone, so the DUT sends no second SUBSCRIBE inside the
  run.

  `test/e2e/run_e2e.sh` reads the transport token of the SUBSCRIBE Via and the
  transport parameter of its Contact off the SIPp message log. Reason: a SIPp
  regexp attribute takes no keyword, so it cannot name the transport that the
  run was given. The scenario needs a tcp or tls DUT with `mwi_subscribe` 1.
-->
<scenario name="UAS registration and message-waiting SUBSCRIBE">
  <recv request="REGISTER|SUBSCRIBE" regexp_match="true" timeout="60000"/>

  <send>
    <![CDATA[
      SIP/2.0 200 OK
      [last_Via:]
      [last_From:]
      [last_To:];tag=sipp-[pid]-[call_number]
      [last_Call-ID:]
      [last_CSeq:]
      [last_Contact:]
      Expires: 3600
      Content-Length: 0

    ]]>
  </send>
</scenario>
